    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. The issue is that my sensors are picking up some really erratic spikes in the salinity readings at around 4000m, which doesn't seem to align with the temperature drops we're seeing. The data looks almost jagged or noisy in certain windows. Could that be a sensor calibration error or is there something else about the local water chemistry that would cause such sudden, non-linear shifts?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those erratic spikes at depth are concerning and could stem from a few issues. A sensor malfunction or drift in the conductivity-temperature-depth (CTD) probe is a primary suspect for jagged readings. Alternatively, localized brine rejection from specific biological processes or rapid methane venting could cause abrupt, non-linear salinity shifts near the seafloor. You should cross-reference the salinity spikes with concurrent pressure and temperature readings to isolate the anomaly.",
      "source": "target",
